                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It's a Mega Man clone featuring cute cyborg anime girls called GALS. The premise is pretty simple: you play as a cyborg girl tasked with saving the world from an evil military leader after your creator and the rest of your sisters are captured. It's up to you to rescue them all.\n\nEveryone says the game is hard, and I can't really disagree. That said, I like to take things slowly, so I still had a good time. Some parts of the level design could definitely use more work, especially when it comes to warning players about bottomless pits or explaining certain gimmicks. At times, the stages feel unforgiving and a bit too long, with too many mechanics packed into a single level. I'm looking at you, Creeper's Stage 3.\n\nThe art style could use some improvement, but honestly, it's not bad.\n\nThe soundtrack is decent, but I think it's mixed too quietly. With all the sound effects going on, it's often hard to hear the music clearly. Creeper's Fortress theme and boss battle theme are among my favorites.\n\nI also ran into a few bugs. During transitions to boss doors, if something is blocking the other side, you can get stuck. I reproduced this in Buster Gal's stage after the mid-boss, but it can also happen elsewhere, such as in the intro/tutorial stage when choosing Warp Gal. Another bug I encountered was in Creeper's Fortress 2, where there was an invisible platform I could stand on.\n\nOne thing I really like is that after beating the game once, you can play as the bosses. It adds a lot of replay value and lets you experiment with different abilities and playstyles that feel completely different from the main protagonist. Some of them are incredibly overpowered.\n\nI enjoyed the game. It's far from perfect, but there's definitely fun to be had if you're willing to give it a chance. I'd say it's worth checking out.",
